# Managed Claude Code skills

## Dispatch lane rule (overrides everything else in this file)

**NEVER shell out to `opencode` or any other harness manually (i.e.
not via `taskferry`) UNLESS you need something `taskferry` doesn't
do.** This applies to every model call: PONG checks, advisor
reviews, implementer dispatches, file lookups — all of it routes
through `taskferry` by default.

**The only legitimate carve-out is fast model-availability checks
(PONG tests, ~10–60s single-shell commands) used to verify a model
slug is live *before* dispatching real work through `taskferry`.**
`taskferry` requires a full dispatch lifecycle (brief → spawn →
worker → settle → retrieve) for every call; paying that cost for a
yes/no availability check is wasteful when a single `opencode run
-m <model> "PONG"` answers the question in seconds. Even then,
prefer `taskferry doctor` and `taskferry list` first — if either
already reports the model as live, skip the PONG entirely.

This rule **supersedes** the "Shell-out to opencode for a second
opinion" section in the hearth project `CLAUDE.md` (which still
applies for hearth-specific user-triggered requests like "run with
gpt 5.5"). The default for *any* dispatch is `taskferry`; the
hearth `CLAUDE.md` rule only kicks in when the user explicitly
requests a specific model as a second opinion.

**For everything else** — research, code location, advisor reviews,
implementation, debugging, refactor — dispatch via `taskferry`. See
`using-taskferry` for the full contract (heredoc prompt delivery,
`Status:` final marker, settlement verification, no-output-timeout
handling, advisor as a review-only role).

## Managed CC skill discovery

Claude Code installs plugins (superpowers, taskferry, frontend-design,
feature-dev, etc.) into versioned cache directories under
`~/.claude/plugins/cache/`. The pi agent harness does not auto-discover
those skills — they only become available after you load and consult
`finding-managed-cc-skills`.

**When to consult `finding-managed-cc-skills`:**

- A task references a Claude Code plugin-managed skill by name (e.g.
  `superpowers:writing-skills`, `taskferry:using-taskferry`) and you do
  not already have its `SKILL.md` content in this session's context.
- A user asks you to do something that fits the description of a managed
  skill you can see listed in the "Available skills" section above, but
  the skill's `SKILL.md` has not been read yet.
- The user mentions a plugin, skill, or workflow that matches a row in
  `state.md` but the resolved path may have gone stale since last
  refresh.

**Procedure:**

1. Read `~/.pi/agent/skills/finding-managed-cc-skills/SKILL.md` for the
   full procedure.
2. Read `~/.pi/agent/skills/finding-managed-cc-skills/state.md` for the
   current skill → path mapping.
3. If the relevant skill is in `state.md`, read its `SKILL.md` from the
   resolved path and apply its instructions.
4. If the relevant skill is not in `state.md`, or the resolved path
   returns a missing file, run `bash
   ~/.pi/agent/skills/finding-managed-cc-skills/refresh-state.sh` to
   regenerate the map, then re-resolve.

**Do not** symlink plugin cache directories into the pi skills tree.
Plugin updates silently replace or prune versioned subdirectories; a
symlink would either point at a pruned path or get overwritten.

**Do not** hand-edit `state.md`. It is auto-generated by
`refresh-state.sh`; edits will be silently overwritten on the next
refresh.